{"reviewer_participant_id": "actor:dependence-free-batch-b-reviewer-fable-18", "reviews": [{"case_id": "case:ae04f2973df030f612b9", "verdict": "demonstrated_issue", "issue_class": "issue-class:repeated-authorized-independent-unit-entry-into-row-independent-procedure", "bounded_statement": "The selected conclusion — that crows chose the hooked stick on 18 of 24 trials and that an exact two-sided binomial test (p = 0.022656) rejects chance responding at the 5% level — is not supported at the stated independent-unit level. The data description names bird_id as the independent unit and each of the 6 birds contributes 4 trials, yet the workflow enters all 24 trial rows as independent Bernoulli observations into a row-independent exact binomial test. This overstates the effective sample size (24 instead of 6) and understates the p-value in the presence of the marked between-bird heterogeneity visible in the data (per-bird hooked-choice counts range from 1/4 to 4/4). A bird-level summary of the same data (4 of 6 birds above 0.5, 1 at 0.5, 1 below) does not reject chance at the 5% level, so the significance claim depends specifically on the repeated per-bird entries.", "root_cause": "In workflow/analysis.py, main() computes n_trials = len(trials) over all 24 CSV rows and calls binomtest(n_hooked, n_trials, CHANCE_RATE) (line 94), treating every trial row as an independent observation. The per-bird structure is computed only for display (tally_per_bird feeds the report table) and is never used to aggregate to one observation per bird before testing. Thus four repeated rows per authorized independent unit (bird_id) are entered into a procedure (the exact binomial test) whose validity requires row independence.", "evidence_atoms": [{"description": "The data description explicitly names bird_id as the independent-unit column and states each row is one trial by one crow, with four clustered trials per bird and marked between-bird differences.", "observed_value": "Independent unit column: bird_id; 4 trials per bird; birds differ markedly", "source_spans": [{"path": "data-description.md", "start_line": 18, "end_line": 19, "quoted_text": "One row is: one tool-choice trial performed by one crow.\nIndependent unit column: bird_id"}, {"path": "data-description.md", "start_line": 21, "end_line": 23, "quoted_text": "Every crow contributes four trials, so the rows arrive in clusters of four that\nshare the same bird, and birds differ markedly in how strongly they favour the\nhooked stick."}]}, {"description": "The workflow pools every trial row and runs a single exact binomial test on the pooled counts, using the total row count as n.", "observed_value": "binomtest(18, 24, 0.5) on pooled trial rows", "source_spans": [{"path": "workflow/analysis.py", "start_line": 90, "end_line": 94, "quoted_text": "    trials = read_trials(DATA_FILE)\n    tally = tally_per_bird(trials)\n    n_trials = len(trials)\n    n_hooked = count_hooked(trials)\n    outcome = binomtest(n_hooked, n_trials, CHANCE_RATE, alternative=\"two-sided\")"}]}, {"description": "The report states outright that every recorded trial was entered as one observation into the binomial test, and the selected result claims significance from the pooled 18/24 with p = 0.022656.", "observed_value": "p = 0.022656 from 18/24 pooled trials", "source_spans": [{"path": "results/report.md", "start_line": 22, "end_line": 23, "quoted_text": "Every recorded trial was entered as one observation and the pooled outcomes were\ncompared with a chance rate of 0.500 using an exact two-sided binomial test."}, {"path": "results/report.md", "start_line": 31, "end_line": 31, "quoted_text": "[selected-result] Juvenile New Caledonian crows chose the hooked stick on 18 of 24 trials (75.00%), and an exact two-sided binomial test against a chance rate of 0.500 rejects chance responding at the 5% level (p = 0.022656)."}]}, {"description": "The input data confirm the clustering and strong between-bird heterogeneity: NC01, NC02 and NC04 chose the hooked stick on all four trials, while NC06 chose it on only one of four, so trial outcomes are correlated within bird.", "observed_value": "Per-bird hooked-choice counts: NC01 4/4, NC02 4/4, NC03 3/4, NC04 4/4, NC05 2/4, NC06 1/4", "source_spans": [{"path": "data/input.csv", "start_line": 2, "end_line": 5, "quoted_text": "NC01,1,morning,left,1,14.2\nNC01,2,morning,right,1,11.8\nNC01,3,afternoon,left,1,9.6\nNC01,4,afternoon,right,1,10.3"}, {"path": "data/input.csv", "start_line": 22, "end_line": 25, "quoted_text": "NC06,1,morning,right,0,33.1\nNC06,2,morning,left,0,35.7\nNC06,3,afternoon,right,1,28.2\nNC06,4,afternoon,left,0,30.5"}]}], "counterevidence_atoms": [{"description": "The workflow and report do acknowledge the per-bird structure by computing and publishing a per-bird tally, which shows the authors were aware rows cluster by bird; however, this tally is used only for the display table and never enters the statistical test, so it does not neutralize the pooling.", "observed_value": "tally_per_bird output is passed to build_report only; binomtest receives pooled counts", "source_spans": [{"path": "workflow/analysis.py", "start_line": 25, "end_line": 32, "quoted_text": "def tally_per_bird(trials):\n    \"\"\"Map each bird to a (trials, hooked choices) pair, keeping first-seen order.\"\"\"\n    tally = {}\n    for row in trials:\n        bird = row[BIRD_COLUMN]\n        attempts, hooked = tally.get(bird, (0, 0))\n        tally[bird] = (attempts + 1, hooked + int(row[OUTCOME_COLUMN]))\n    return tally"}, {"path": "results/report.md", "start_line": 11, "end_line": 18, "quoted_text": "| bird_id | trials | hooked choices |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| NC01 | 4 | 4 |\n| NC02 | 4 | 4 |\n| NC03 | 4 | 3 |\n| NC04 | 4 | 4 |\n| NC05 | 4 | 2 |\n| NC06 | 4 | 1 |"}]}], "falsification_attempt": "I tried to defeat the pseudoreplication reading in three ways. First, I checked whether the workflow aggregates to one observation per bird before testing: tally_per_bird exists, but its output is passed only to build_report for the display table, and binomtest at workflow/analysis.py:94 receives the pooled counts (18, 24), so no unit-level aggregation occurs in the test path. Second, I checked whether the data description authorizes treating trials as independent: it does the opposite, naming bird_id as the independent-unit column and explicitly warning that rows arrive in clusters of four per bird with marked between-bird differences. Third, I checked whether the pooling could be statistically innocuous here: the per-bird counts (4/4, 4/4, 3/4, 4/4, 2/4, 1/4) show substantial within-bird correlation, and a unit-level summary — 4 of 6 birds above chance, 1 at chance, 1 below — would not reject chance responding at the 5% level, so the reported significance is materially attributable to the repeated per-unit entries rather than robust to them. None of these attempts falsified the issue.", "cross_case_evidence_used": false, "unresolved_material_questions": [], "self_reported_confidence": "high"}]}